\begin{note}
The default \lstinline{__eq__} for objects is identity:
two distinct objects are never equal.
Python also gives every object a default \lstinline{__hash__} based on its identity.
There is a contract between \lstinline{__eq__} and \lstinline{__hash__}:
if two objects are equal, they must have the same hash.
When you override \lstinline{__eq__},
python automatically sets \lstinline{__hash__ = None},
which makes the class unhashable.
This is to prevent the class from silently violating the contract.
\end{note}
